# intel-ucode: Intel processor microcode for early loading

## What this package is

The official Intel microcode bundle (release microcode-20260925),
shipped two ways:

- `/lib/firmware/intel-ucode/*` - the per-signature blobs, verbatim
  and unmodified, for the late loader
  (`echo 1 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/microcode/reload`) and for
  inspection. `intel-ucode-with-caveats/` from upstream is NOT
  shipped (those MCUs need kernel late-loader patches Saphira does
  not carry - see Intel's README).
- `/boot/intel-ucode.img` - the early-loading image: one
  UNCOMPRESSED cpio holding the concatenated bundle as
  `kernel/x86/microcode/GenuineIntel.bin`. The kernel scans it
  before normal initialisation and applies the entry matching the
  running CPU. GRUB's stock early-initrd list already names
  `intel-ucode.img`, so no bootloader configuration is needed
  beyond the grub.cfg regeneration this package's hooks trigger.

Microcode is volatile: it resets on power loss, so it must load on
EVERY boot. Installing this package alone does NOT activate
anything - activation is the initrd line in grub.cfg plus a reboot.

## Verification (physical cold boot)

1. `intel-ucode-status` - CPU signature, loaded revision, dmesg
   early-loader lines, image and bundle presence.
2. `dmesg | grep -i microcode` must show an early update line and a
   revision at/after Intel's published revision for the signature.
3. `cat /proc/cpuinfo | grep microcode | sort | uniq` agrees.

## Licence

Intel redistribution licence (binary form, unmodified), shipped
verbatim as `/usr/share/licenses/intel-ucode/license`. The cpio
wrapping and bundle concatenation are the kernel-documented early
container, not a modification of the blobs.
